%% child_agent.vglp — the CSSN child agent as a vGLP program.
%%
%% vGLP source of the tested GLP program child_agent.glp + ui/mediator.glp (this
%% directory): the child agent authored with volition-guarded clauses, the
%% mediator and its UserCmd/UserNotify vocabulary absent — they belong to the
%% implementation, which maps the volition-guarded clauses into concrete UI
%% constructs.  Exhibit for the paper "Volition Elicitation: Operational
%% Semantics for People and Their Machines", which defines vGLP.  agent.vglp is
%% the adult agent; the two files together are the platform's vGLP program, and
%% A5 of the cascade is that program.
%%
%% NOTATION (defined in the paper):
%%   *                          marks a volition-guarded clause: reducing a goal with
%%                              it requires, besides its guards, the person willing
%%                              this reduction. Among volition-guarded clauses for
%%                              the same goal, the person's answer selects the clause.
%%   *(X1,...,Xk)               a volition-guarded clause with person-supplied inputs:
%%                              the person's answer binds the writers X1..Xk; the clause
%%                              carries their readers Xi?.
%%
%% WHY EVERY ACT OF THE CHILD'S IS GUARDED HERE, which is the claim the paper
%% rests on and was measured rather than assumed (CSSN Code, 2026-08-16 14:22).
%% Twenty clauses of child_agent/6 are triggered by msg(person, Id1, ...), the
%% person's own command, and each is a volition-guarded clause below.  Three
%% arriving offers only notify and wait — child_intro from the parent,
%% group_invite from the parent, and group_invite from a friend, which asks the
%% parent first — and each is a responder.  In a run of fplay13 each offer
%% arrives as a card the child answers and each act is a form the child issues.
%%
%% THREE CLAUSES CARRY NO VOLITION GUARD, AND THAT IS CORRECT.  The parent
%% telling this child to leave a group, the parent telling it to unfriend, and a
%% friend unfriending it change this child's state without asking it.  Those are
%% exactly the acts the specification guards by any ONE of the parties, where
%% this child is not a guard: an unguarded clause here is the absence of a guard
%% in the transaction, not an omission in the elicitation.
%%
%% TWO THINGS THIS FILE CARRIES THAT THE ADULT EXHIBIT DOES NOT.
%%   - THE PARENT ROLE ENTIRE.  A child may itself become a parent: becoming one
%%     asks nothing of the party that becomes one, and no clause of a grassroots
%%     contract may gate the act by which two strangers come into relation.  So
%%     the offering, the adoption of the channel the child conveys, the ending of
%%     the parenting, and the counts its own children's acts leave here are all
%%     below.  Its own parent is reached by send_parent and its children by
%%     send_child; rho_r and kappa_r are different entries of the outputs list.
%%   - THE HAND-OVER AT BOTH ENDS.  The parenting beginning is where agent hands
%%     to child_agent, and its ending is where child_agent hands back.  The
%%     ending is a clause of this file and is the one place a child agent stops
%%     being one; the beginning is agent.vglp's, and neither exhibit is complete
%%     without the other.
%%
%% RESPONDERS.  An arriving offer spawns a responder goal — respond_child_intro,
%% respond_group_invite, respond_child_consent, respond_release — one goal per
%% offer, so offers pend concurrently and are answered in any order.  The
%% responder carries the offer and the escrowed writer or channel; its
%% volition-guarded sibling clauses are the person's choice, and the chosen
%% clause acts — directly on the escrowed channel, or by injecting its answer,
%% through a merge, into the agent's UserIn.
%%
%% TWO ANSWER CLAUSES BELOW HAVE NO RESPONDER, and that is a gap in the deployed
%% code rather than in this exhibit.  child_agent.glp carries
%% approve_child_intro/3 and approve_group/3 as person commands — the parent's
%% approval of an introduction, and of a group invitation, for a child of its own
%% — but it has NO clause receiving child_intro from a friend or
%% group_invite_child at all, where agent.glp has both.  So a party that is both
%% a child and a parent can approve neither: nothing can deliver it the offer.
%% The answer clauses are written here because they are in the deployed code and
%% the correspondence is clause for clause; the responders are absent because the
%% offers are.  Reported to CSSN Cowork.
%%
%% Correspondence to the implementation (clause for clause):
%%   - a *(X1..Xk) Request clause  ->  a UserCmd constructor over X1..Xk, a mediator
%%     pass-through, and the child_agent clause matching msg(person, Id, cmd(X1..Xk)):
%%     send/2, child_introduce/3, create_group/1, invite_group/2, send_group/2,
%%     leave_group/1, leave_child_group/2, unfriend_child/1, unfriend_child_of/2
%%     and enrol_child/1; and request_release, whose Request clause takes no
%%     input because the asking IS the act;
%%   - a responder goal, spawned per arriving offer  ->  a pending-table entry
%%     req(N) in the mediator, rendered as an inbox card;
%%   - a responder's volition-guarded clause  ->  the UserCmds accept_child_intro/2
%%     and reject_child_intro/2 (an introduction from the parent), accept_group/2
%%     and reject_group/2 (a group invitation), approve_child_group/3 and
%%     reject_child_group/3 (a consent request from a child of its own), and
%%     approve_release/1 (a child of its own asking that the parenting end),
%%     routing the escrowed writer or channel back to the agent.
%% The escrow vocabulary — child_befriend/3, the agent-to-user group_invite/2,
%% child_group_consent/3, release_asked/1, and the pending table — does not
%% appear here: the machine's question to the person is not a message but a
%% pending volition-guarded reduction.
%%
%% This file is vGLP, not GLP: the REPL does not load it (hence .vglp).  Its
%% correctness argument is the clause-for-clause correspondence to the tested,
%% deployed implementation in this directory.
%%
%% Shared types (AgentState, EnrolResponse, Response, Decision, Handshake,
%% IntroContent, FriendContent, FriendMsg, GroupId, GroupContent, GroupMsg,
%% GroupSetupResponse, OutputKey, NetColdCall, NetInMsg, NetInStream, NetOutMsg,
%% NetOutStream, IntroResult, GroupJoinResult, GroupPost, SentEntry) are those of
%% self.glp.  Helper procedures — channel ops, merge, the lib/routing routers
%% (send_user, send_friend, send_child, send_parent, send_net), intro_await_peer,
%% inject_intro_result, befriend_commit, close_friend, the self.glp group helpers
%% (setup_group_creator, setup_group_hub_side, lookup_send_group,
%% distribute_group_msg, add_group_output, group_await_join, inject_group_result,
%% close_output, close_outputs), the enrolment helpers (add_parent_output,
%% close_parent, close_child, enrol_commit, inject_enrol_response), add_post, and
%% the local await_group_channel, child_consent_wait, decline_intro and
%% decline_enrol — are those of
%% child_agent.glp, self.glp and lib/routing/, unchanged; this file gives the
%% child_agent procedure and its responders.


%% Screen vocabulary — self.glp's AgentContent minus the ask vocabulary (of
%% AgentOnlyContent: child_befriend/3, child_group_consent/3, release_asked/1;
%% of GroupNotifyContent: group_invite/2 — group_removed/1 stays): under vGLP the
%% machine asks the person by a pending reduction, not by a message.
AgentContent ::= connected(Constant)
               ; rejected(Constant)
               ; received(Constant, Constant)
               ; group_joined(GroupId)
               ; group_joined_member(GroupId, Constant)
               ; group_left(GroupId)
               ; group_received(GroupId, Constant, Constant)
               ; group_removed(GroupId)
               ; unfriended(Constant)
               ; enrolled(Constant)
               ; released(Constant).
AgentToUserMsg ::= msg(Constant, Constant, AgentContent).

%% UserIn — under vGLP the person's commands are grants, not messages; UserIn
%% carries the answers the responders inject and the results injected by
%% inject_intro_result, inject_group_result and inject_enrol_response.
UserInMsg   ::= accept_child_intro(Constant, Channel(Stream(IntroContent), Stream(IntroContent)))
              ; approve_child_intro(Constant, Constant, Channel(Stream(IntroContent), Stream(IntroContent)))
              ; accept_group(GroupId, Stream(GroupSetupResponse))
              ; approve_group(Constant, GroupId, Channel(Stream(IntroContent), Stream(GroupSetupResponse)))
              ; intro_result(Constant, Channel(Stream(IntroContent), Stream(IntroContent)))
              ; intro_rejected(Constant)
              ; group_join_ack(GroupId, Constant, Channel(Stream(IntroContent), Stream(GroupSetupResponse)))
              ; group_join_nack(GroupId, Constant)
              ; enrol_response(Constant, EnrolResponse)
              ; approve_release(Constant).

OutputEntry ::= user_output(Stream(AgentToUserMsg)?)
              ; net_output(NetOutStream?)
              ; friend_output(Constant, Stream(FriendMsg)?)
              ; child_output(Constant, Stream(FriendMsg)?)
              ; parent_output(Constant, Stream(FriendMsg)?)
              ; group_entry(OutputKey, Stream(GroupMsg)?).

%% The offers the responders carry: the question the pending reduction poses.
ChildIntroOffer  ::= offer(Constant).
GroupInviteOffer ::= offer(GroupId).
ConsentOffer     ::= offer(Constant, GroupId).
ReleaseOffer     ::= offer(Constant).

%% =============================================================================
%% RESPONDERS — one goal per arriving offer; the volition-guarded clauses live
%% here, so offers pend concurrently and are answered in any order.
%% =============================================================================

%% An introduction the child's parent has passed on.  A child's friendships are
%% established through parental introductions, so this is the only way one
%% reaches it, and the child's own will is the other half of the act.
procedure respond_child_intro(Constant?, ChildIntroOffer?, Channel(Stream(IntroContent), Stream(IntroContent))?, Stream(UserInMsg)).

%% Accept: ack on the setup channel, await the peer, inject the result.
*(Answer=yes, Other?)
respond_child_intro(Id, offer(Other), Ch, Ans?) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Other?),
    send(ack(Id?), Ch?, Ch1) |
    intro_await_peer(Other?, Ch1?, Result),
    inject_intro_result(Result?, [], Ans).

%% Reject: nack on the setup channel, closing it.
*(Answer=no, Other?)
respond_child_intro(_, offer(Other), ch(_ChIn, [nack|[]]), []) :-
    ground(Other?) | true.

%% A group invitation.  It reaches the child two ways and this responder serves
%% both: forwarded by the parent, who has already consented, or sent by a friend,
%% in which case the parent's consent has just been given on the parent-child
%% channel.  Either way the child is asked, and this is where it answers.
procedure respond_group_invite(Constant?, GroupInviteOffer?, Channel(Stream(GroupSetupResponse), Stream(Handshake))?, Stream(UserInMsg)).

%% Accept: ack on the setup channel, hand the read side to the agent to await
%% the group channel from the hub.
*(Answer=yes, GroupId?)
respond_group_invite(Id, offer(GroupId), ch(ChRead, [ack(Id?)|[]]),
    [accept_group(GroupId?, ChRead?)]) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?) | true.

%% Reject: nack on the setup channel, closing it.
*(Answer=no, GroupId?)
respond_group_invite(_, offer(GroupId), ch(_ChRead, [nack|[]]), []) :-
    ground(GroupId?) | true.

%% A consent request from a child of this party's own — the parent role, held by
%% a party that is itself a child.  The Decision it binds is read by the asking
%% child, which is blocked on it.
procedure respond_child_consent(ConsentOffer?, Decision).

%% Grant the child's consent request.
*(Answer=yes, Child?, GroupId?)
respond_child_consent(offer(Child, GroupId), Answer?) :-
    ground(Child?), ground(GroupId?) | true.

%% Refuse.
*(Answer=no, Child?, GroupId?)
respond_child_consent(offer(Child, GroupId), Answer?) :-
    ground(Child?), ground(GroupId?) | true.

%% A child of this party's own asking that the parenting end.  The parent owes
%% the answer, and a refusal is an act not taken: the declining clause has no
%% body, so nothing records it.
procedure respond_release(ReleaseOffer?, Stream(UserInMsg)).

%% Grant it.
*(Answer=yes, Child?)
respond_release(offer(Child), [approve_release(Child?)]) :-
    ground(Child?) | true.

%% Withhold it.
*(Answer=no, Child?)
respond_release(offer(Child), []) :-
    ground(Child?) | true.

%% =============================================================================
%% CHILD_AGENT/6
%% =============================================================================

procedure child_agent(Constant?, Constant?, AgentState?, Stream(UserInMsg)?, Stream(NetInMsg)?, Stream(OutputEntry)?).

%% --- Volitional: ending the parenting ---

%% Ask the parent to end it.  The Request clause takes no input from the person:
%% the asking IS the act, and which parent is not a choice, a child having one.
*
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(ParentId?) |
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, release_request), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% --- Volitional: the parent role, which a child may hold too ---

%% Offer to become a parent of another party.  It asks nothing of that party's
%% standing, and no clause may gate it: this is the act by which two strangers
%% come into relation.
*(Child)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Child?) |
    send_net(msg(Child?, enrol(Id?, Resp)), Outs?, Outs1),
    inject_enrol_response(Resp?, Child?, UserIn?, UserIn1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn1?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% Introduce two children of our own to each other (the sibling case).
*(Child1, Friend, Child2)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), Friend? =?= Id?, ground(Child1?), ground(Child2?),
    new_channel(C1Ch, C2Ch) |
    send_child(Child1?, msg(Id?, Child1?, child_intro(Child2?, C1Ch?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    send_child(Child2?, msg(Id?, Child2?, child_intro(Child1?, C2Ch?)), Outs1?, Outs2),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs2?).

%% Introduce a child of our own to a child of a friend.  The deployed clause
%% uses the same explicit negation this one does, not `otherwise`: an
%% `otherwise` here would wait on the clauses reading this child's own network
%% input.
*(MyChild, Friend, FriendChild)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ~(Friend? =?= Id?),
    ground(MyChild?), ground(Friend?), ground(FriendChild?),
    new_channel(C1Ch, C2Ch) |
    send_child(MyChild?, msg(Id?, MyChild?, child_intro(FriendChild?, C1Ch?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    send_friend(Friend?, msg(Id?, Friend?, child_intro(MyChild?, C2Ch?)), Outs1?, Outs2),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs2?).

%% Tell a child of our own to leave a group.
*(Child, GroupId)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Child?), ground(GroupId?) |
    send_child(Child?, msg(Id?, Child?, leave_group(GroupId?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% End a friendship of a child of our own.  Nothing is looked up and nothing
%% checked: our approvals name nobody, which is what lets any parent of a child
%% end any friendship of that child.
*(MyChild, Target)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(MyChild?), ground(Target?) |
    send_child(MyChild?, msg(Id?, MyChild?, unfriend_child(Target?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% --- Volitional: what the child does as a child ---

%% Message a friend.
*(Target, Text)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Target?) |
    send_friend(Target?, msg(Id?, Target?, text(Text?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% Create a group.
*(Name)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Name?) |
    setup_group_creator(group_id(Id?, Name?), Outs?, Outs1, NetIn?, NetIn1),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, group_joined(group_id(Id?, Name?))), Outs1?, Outs2),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn1?, Outs2?).

%% Offer group membership to a friend.
*(GroupId, Invitee)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?), ground(Invitee?),
    new_channel(InviteeCh, CreatorCh) |
    send_friend(Invitee?, msg(Id?, Invitee?, group_invite(GroupId?, InviteeCh?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    group_await_join(GroupId?, Invitee?, CreatorCh?, Result),
    inject_group_result(Result?, UserIn?, UserIn1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn1?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% Post to a group — send on the member-to-hub channel.
*(GroupId, Text)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?), ground(Text?) |
    lookup_send_group(group(GroupId?),
        msg(Id?, Id?, group_msg(GroupId?, Id?, Text?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% Remove a member from a group of ours.  This is the creator's role of
%% child_leave_2: a child that created a group is one of the four who may end a
%% child's membership of it.  Our own parent takes a withdrawal, the removed
%% child's parent taking the other when group_removed reaches it.
*(GroupId, Member)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?), ground(Member?) |
    lookup_send_group(group_member(GroupId?, Member?),
        msg(Id?, Member?, group_removed(GroupId?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    close_output(group_member(GroupId?, Member?), Outs1?, Outs2),
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, withdrawal), Outs2?, Outs3),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs3?).

%% Leave a group.  A child's leaving is guarded by any one of the creator, the
%% child and a parent of it, and advances that parent's withdrawal count
%% whichever of them willed it — here it is the child.
*(GroupId)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?) |
    lookup_send_group(group(GroupId?),
        msg(Id?, Id?, group_leave(GroupId?, Id?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    close_output(group(GroupId?), Outs1?, Outs2),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, group_left(GroupId?)), Outs2?, Outs3),
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, withdrawal), Outs3?, Outs4),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs4?).

%% End a friendship of our own.  The parent is told, and its withdrawal count
%% advances; it names nobody, so nothing it holds says which friendship ended.
*(Target)
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Target?) |
    send_friend(Target?, msg(Id?, Target?, unfriend), Outs?, Outs1),
    close_friend(Target?, Outs1?, Outs2),
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, withdrawal), Outs2?, Outs3),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, unfriended(Target?)), Outs3?, Outs4),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs4?).

%% --- Reactive: network messages — arriving offers spawn responders ---

%% The parent passes on an introduction: spawn a responder — the offer pends
%% until the person answers; the answer is merged into UserIn.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(From, Id1, child_intro(Other, Ch))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, From? =?= ParentId?, ground(Other?) |
    respond_child_intro(Id?, offer(Other?), Ch?, Ans),
    merge(Ans?, UserIn?, UserIn1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn1?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% The parent forwards a group invitation, having already consented: spawn a
%% responder likewise.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(From, Id1, group_invite(GroupId, Ch))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, From? =?= ParentId?, ground(GroupId?) |
    respond_group_invite(Id?, offer(GroupId?), Ch?, Ans),
    merge(Ans?, UserIn?, UserIn1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn1?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% A friend sends a group invitation: the parent's consent is asked first, on the
%% parent-child channel, and child_consent_wait spawns the responder if it comes.
%% Two persons' wills, in order, and the child's is the second.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(From, Id1, group_invite(GroupId, Ch))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, ~(From? =?= ParentId?), ground(GroupId?) |
    send_parent(ParentId?,
        msg(Id?, ParentId?, group_consent_req(GroupId?, Consent)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_consent_wait(Consent?, Id?, ParentId?, St?, GroupId?, Ch?,
        UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% A child of ours asks that the parenting end: spawn a responder.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(Child, Id1, release_request)|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, ground(Child?) |
    respond_release(offer(Child?), Ans),
    merge(Ans?, UserIn?, UserIn1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn1?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% A child of ours asks our consent for a group invitation it received: spawn a
%% responder, which binds the Decision the asking child is blocked on.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(Child, Id1, group_consent_req(GroupId, Consent?))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, ground(Child?), ground(GroupId?) |
    respond_child_consent(offer(Child?, GroupId?), Consent),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% --- Reactive: acts this child is not a guard of ---
%% These three change this child's state without asking it, and the
%% specification guards each by any ONE of the parties, this child not among
%% them.  An unguarded clause here is the absence of a guard in the transaction.

%% The parent tells this child to leave a group.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(From, Id1, leave_group(GroupId))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, From? =?= ParentId?, ground(GroupId?) |
    lookup_send_group(group(GroupId?),
        msg(Id?, Id?, group_leave(GroupId?, Id?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    close_output(group(GroupId?), Outs1?, Outs2),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, group_left(GroupId?)), Outs2?, Outs3),
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, withdrawal), Outs3?, Outs4),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs4?).

%% The parent tells this child to unfriend.  ANY parent of either child may will
%% it, so the parent that sent it need not be the one that approved the
%% friendship — and this child's own parent is the one whose withdrawal count
%% advances.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(From, Id1, unfriend_child(Target))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, From? =?= ParentId?, ground(Target?) |
    send_friend(Target?, msg(Id?, Target?, unfriend), Outs?, Outs1),
    close_friend(Target?, Outs1?, Outs2),
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, withdrawal), Outs2?, Outs3),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, unfriended(Target?)), Outs3?, Outs4),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs4?).

%% A friend unfriends this child.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(From, Id1, unfriend)|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, ground(From?) |
    close_friend(From?, Outs?, Outs1),
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, withdrawal), Outs1?, Outs2),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, unfriended(From?)), Outs2?, Outs3),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs3?).

%% The creator removes this child from a group — the third party that may will a
%% child's leaving, and it advances the parent's withdrawal count like the other
%% two.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(_, Id1, group_removed(GroupId))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, ground(GroupId?) |
    close_output(group(GroupId?), Outs?, Outs1),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, group_removed(GroupId?)), Outs1?, Outs2),
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, withdrawal), Outs2?, Outs3),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs3?).

%% --- Reactive: cold calls a child does not take ---

%% A child's friendships are established through parental introductions, so a
%% cold call is declined without troubling the person.  A child has a network
%% entry because an enrolment offer has nowhere else to reach it.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(Id1, intro(_, Resp?))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1? |
    decline_intro(Resp),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% A party already holding a parent declines a further offer of one.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(Id1, enrol(_, Resp?))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1? |
    decline_enrol(Resp),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% --- Reactive: what a child of ours reports, and what the group carries ---

%% A friendship or membership of a child of ours has been established, or ended.
%% The counts name nobody, which is what makes any parent of a child able to end
%% any friendship or membership of that child.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, state(Al, Om, Ps, Sn), UserIn, [msg(_, Id1, approval)|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1? |
    Al1 := Al? + 1,
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, state(Al1?, Om?, Ps?, Sn?), UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs?).

child_agent(Id, ParentId, state(Al, Om, Ps, Sn), UserIn, [msg(_, Id1, withdrawal)|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1? |
    Om1 := Om? + 1,
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, state(Al?, Om1?, Ps?, Sn?), UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% A friend sends text.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(From, Id1, text(Text))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1? |
    send_user(msg(agent, person, received(From?, Text?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% A post arrives on a group channel: hold it, distribute it to the members on
%% the list now, and pass it to the interface.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, state(Al, Om, Ps, Sn), UserIn, [msg(_, _, group_msg(GroupId, Author, Text))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?), ground(Author?), ground(Text?) |
    add_post(post(GroupId?, Author?, Text?), Ps?, Ps1),
    distribute_group_msg(GroupId?,
        msg(Author?, Author?, group_msg(GroupId?, Author?, Text?)), Sn?, Sn1, Outs?, Outs1),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, group_received(GroupId?, Author?, Text?)), Outs1?, Outs2),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, state(Al?, Om?, Ps1?, Sn1?), UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs2?).

%% The creator of a group of ours is told a member has left.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(Member, _, group_leave(GroupId, Member1))|NetIn], Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?), ground(Member?), Member? =?= Member1? |
    close_output(group_member(GroupId?, Member?), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% --- Reactive: the answers and injected results on UserIn ---

%% This child's acceptance of an introduction, from respond_child_intro: the
%% peer acked, so commit and tell the parent, whose approval is part of the same
%% transaction.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [intro_result(Other, Ch)|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Other?) |
    befriend_commit(Id?, Other?, Ch?, Outs?, Outs1, NetIn?, NetIn1),
    send_parent(ParentId?, msg(Id?, ParentId?, approval), Outs1?, Outs2),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn1?, Outs2?).

%% The peer nacked.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [intro_rejected(Other)|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?) |
    send_user(msg(agent, person, rejected(Other?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% This child's acceptance of a group invitation, from respond_group_invite:
%% await the group channel from the hub.  The membership commits there, and a
%% parent of the child is a party to it, so the parent's approval count advances
%% at the commit and naming nobody.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [accept_group(GroupId, ChRead)|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?) |
    await_group_channel(Id?, ParentId?, St?, GroupId?, ChRead?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% A group of ours has a new member: wire the hub side, and write on it every
%% post of the group we hold — the joiner is owed them.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, state(Al, Om, Ps, Sn), [group_join_ack(GroupId, Invitee, ch(_, [group_channel(MemberEnd?)|[]]))|UserIn],
        NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?), ground(Invitee?) |
    setup_group_hub_side(GroupId?, Invitee?, Ps?, Ps1, Sn?, Sn1, Outs?, Outs1, NetIn?, NetIn1, MemberEnd),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, group_joined_member(GroupId?, Invitee?)), Outs1?, Outs2),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, state(Al?, Om?, Ps1?, Sn1?), UserIn?, NetIn1?, Outs2?).

%% The invitee declined.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [group_join_nack(_, _)|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?) |
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs?).

%% The party we offered to parent accepted: adopt the channel it conveyed.
%% kappa_r gains it.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [enrol_response(Child, enrol_accept(Ch))|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Child?) |
    enrol_commit(Child?, Ch?, Outs?, Outs1, NetIn?, NetIn1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn1?, Outs1?).

%% It declined.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [enrol_response(Child, enrol_no)|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?) |
    send_user(msg(agent, person, rejected(Child?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% Our grant of a release, from respond_release: grant it on the parent-child
%% channel, then tear that channel down.  kappa_r loses the child.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [approve_release(Child)|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(Child?) |
    send_child(Child?, msg(Id?, Child?, release_grant), Outs?, Outs1),
    close_child(Child?, Outs1?, Outs2),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, released(Child?)), Outs2?, Outs3),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs3?).

%% Our approval of an introduction for a child of our own — forward it on.  NO
%% RESPONDER YIELDS THIS TODAY: see the note in the header.  child_agent.glp has
%% no clause receiving child_intro from a friend, so nothing can deliver the
%% offer to a party that is both a child and a parent.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [approve_child_intro(OtherChild, MyChild, Ch)|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(OtherChild?), ground(MyChild?) |
    send_child(MyChild?, msg(Id?, MyChild?, child_intro(OtherChild?, Ch?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% Our approval of a group invitation for a child of our own — forward it on.
%% NO RESPONDER YIELDS THIS TODAY either: child_agent.glp has no clause
%% receiving group_invite_child.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, [approve_group(Child, GroupId, Ch)|UserIn], NetIn, Outs) :-
    ground(Id?), ground(GroupId?), ground(Child?) |
    send_child(Child?, msg(Id?, Child?, group_invite(GroupId?, Ch?)), Outs?, Outs1),
    child_agent(Id?, ParentId?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs1?).

%% --- Reactive: the hand-over back ---

%% The parenting is ended.  rho_r loses the parent and so is empty, which IS
%% being an adult: the agent is an adult agent from here, and nothing is counted
%% or declared to make it one.  That is what lets it befriend with no parent
%% among the guards.  This is the one clause where a child agent stops being one;
%% agent.vglp carries the other end.
child_agent(Id, ParentId, St, UserIn, [msg(From, Id1, release_grant)|NetIn], Outs) :-
    Id? =?= Id1?, From? =?= ParentId? |
    close_parent(ParentId?, Outs?, Outs1),
    send_user(msg(agent, person, released(ParentId?)), Outs1?, Outs2),
    agent(Id?, St?, UserIn?, NetIn?, Outs2?).

%% --- Termination ---

child_agent(_, _, _, [], _, Outs) :- close_outputs(Outs?).
child_agent(_, _, _, _, [], Outs) :- close_outputs(Outs?).
